From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Rui Figueira <rfigueira@cloudflare.com>
Date: Sat, 25 Jul 2026 06:41:34 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Add dims-only image loads for out-of-process rendering

DocumentConfig::dims_only_images routes every ImageHandler through a
header-only dimension sniff (image crate into_dimensions; no RGBA
decode) and stores the original encoded bytes in the node's replaced
slot via Resource::EncodedImage -> RasterImageData{encoded:true}. Layout
only ever reads width/height, so intrinsic sizing is exact (better than
the decode-cap path, which downscales dims too).

Why: an embedder can paint in a separate process or isolate. Decoded
RGBA is the dominant page heap cost (4000x3000 jpeg: ~2 MB encoded vs
~48 MB decoded), and shipping encoded bytes also beats re-encoding the
pixels for transport on both size and CPU.

A local consumer that does need pixels (canvas drawImage of an <img>)
calls kitesurf_materialize_node_image to decode in place, damage-marked.
It decodes at the node's laid-out size the way Chrome decodes at need:
the final_layout box is the hint, quantized to the largest power-of-two
downscale of the header dims that still covers it. The ceiling parameter
only guards the extreme (a GPU max-texture analog), not quality.
Zero-box nodes (display:none, not laid out) decode at header dims,
ceiling-capped. Undecodable bytes drop to ImageData::None so vello never
sees garbage.

An embedder that has already sniffed the dimensions can also keep the
payload out of the document's process entirely. That matters for
cross-origin images when page JS runs next to the document: bytes there
are bytes the page can read. kitesurf_set_pending_image stages a
resource key and, optionally, dimensions immediately around the
synchronous handler.bytes() call that parses a delivery. Non-zero
dimensions mean the payload is absent by design, so the dims-only parse
skips the header sniff and stores an empty encoded entry carrying just
w/h plus the key.

The key rides in the Blob id via Blob::from_raw_parts, so it reaches the
paint brush with no new field and no change to any trait signature,
which lets a consumer holding no bytes name the resource it is asking
for. Zero keeps Blob::new's counter id and the embedder's existing
content-hash identity.

Materialization sniffs the container dims before its full decode and
drops the image to None when the full-resolution RGBA plane would
exceed 128 MiB (~32 Mpx), the renderer's own decode budget: the encoded
bytes are page-controlled and a tiny file can declare dims that exhaust
memory on decode. Paint skips a None slot, the same degradation as
undecodable bytes.
